\DOC ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS

\TYPE {ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS : thm -> (thm -> tactic) -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves the current goal outright from a supplied theorem together with a
user-supplied continuation tactic, leaving no subgoals.

\DESCRIBE
{th : thm} is the theorem from which the goal is to be derived — a correctness
statement closely matching the goal; {k : thm -> tactic} is a continuation that
is handed a theorem and produces the tactic doing the actual work (in the
observed traces, continuations that specialise and {MP_TAC} the inner theorem,
rewrite with calling-convention and {MAYCHANGE} definitions, and discharge side
conditions). Applied to a goal, it closes it completely:
{
       A ?- t
   ============ ADD_IBT_OPEN_EXISTS th k
}
{
   
}

\COMMENTS
In every observed trace the conclusion is a universally quantified correctness
statement, sometimes wrapped in an outer existential, and none of the goals
carry assumptions, so nothing can be said about assumption handling. The traces
show only this single outcome — the goal being closed on goals of this one
shape.

\ENDDOC
